WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:08.364
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.911 --> 00:00:11.660
DAVE WILLIAMS: Doctor
Edwards Deming said,

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:15.060
if you can't describe work as a
process, you can't improve it.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.310
One of the most powerful and
accessible process improvement

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:22.090
tools we have to learn with is
something called a process map.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:27.370
A process map is a visual display of
the activities of a process or a system.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.150
Process maps are extremely
useful and helpful

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.100
for understanding how
a current process is

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.800
designed by enabling you to quickly
identify opportunities for improvement.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.280
Once a process has been
improved, process maps

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.060
can also act as guidance to
support people doing standard work

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:46.270
and following a designed
process reliably.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:49.940
Process mapping is a powerful
exercise for learning and improving.

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:53.150
I often describe it as one of the
secret weapons for helping teams

00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:58.690
to identify variation and potential
failure points in their work processes.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:01.520
In an ideal scenario, I
like to do process mapping

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:05.660
with a mixed group of stakeholders of
the process we're trying to improve.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.830
I also strive to go to where work
happens versus doing it in a conference

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:12.570
room or a meeting space.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.790
I begin by inviting the team to
think about the average patient

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:20.770
or someone who represents the
normal population this process must

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:22.060
serve every day.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.530
Then I ask them to attempt to describe
the process at a macro level, as

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:29.300
high a level as possible.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:34.840
So let's take an example here
and draw just a simple diagram.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.602
So usually, every process has a start.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.690
And the start and the end often
use a shape that looks like a pill.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.540
And then what you do is
you start to ask yourself,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.670
what are each of the
steps in the process?

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:54.380
And so we might go and say,
well, here is step one.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.320
Put a box there.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.210
And step two.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.280
And step three.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.770
And maybe after step
three, there's actually

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.280
a decision that has to be made.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.510
So we reflect that by
making it a diamond,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:10.690
and that may bring us to an
extra process step down here.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:16.070
So if yes, for example, it may continue
on-- I'll put a little Y here, or just

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.570
write a yes-- it'll continue
on down the process.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:21.570
If it's no, then there
might be a decision

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:25.200
to go down here and fix something
before the process continues.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.820
And then we keep going until we
reach the end, and again, at the end,

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.690
we often describe it as a pill.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.290
So each step along the way, right.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:35.846
We've got process step
one and two and three

00:02:35.846 --> 00:02:38.720
and then there was a decision that
had to be made, and so that either

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.840
takes us to step four or drops
us down here to this step

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:44.570
before we move up to four.

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:48.680
But it's basically a
simple visual display

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.360
of the steps in a process
from start to finish.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.100
Now, in my experience,
most teams won't make it

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.500
past the first step or the
second step in a process

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.370
before they start describing
multiple ways a step can be done,

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:08.570
or they disagree on what the step
is or identify a failure point.

00:03:08.570 --> 00:03:11.500
All of this is fantastic for
generating improvement ideas.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:15.460
This dialogue and listening
here is essential for learning,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.910
and I try to balance
keeping people focused

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:22.060
on getting to a first draft process and
honoring and capturing all of the input

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:23.500
from team members.

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.560
There are a number of
different approaches

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.900
you can take in doing a process map.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.220
From the very basic
hand-drawn flow diagram

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:36.000
on a whiteboard or piece of paper like
I just did, to more complex variations,

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.780
including value stream analysis,
spaghetti diagrams, swim lane diagrams.

00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:45.850
Each helps us understand better
how work actually happens.

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.440
Process mapping is a great tool.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.110
Think about a process you want
to better understand and see

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:54.500
if you can map it out
from start to finish.

